    summary: "Build mode serializes collected module records and assets; runtime mode loads the bundle without build plugins."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Build Without Evaluation" }
    :markdown
      Build mode collects entrypoints and runtime dependencies, waits for generated assets or writes them to disk when requested, and serializes runtime module specs with Klenod's JSON bundle format. It should not evaluate application top-level code.

      ```ruby
      bundle = context.build(
        entrypoints: ["entrypoint"],
        output: "dist/klenod.bundle",
        assets_dir: "dist/public"
      )
      ```

  %DocsSection{ title: "CLI And Config" }
    :markdown
      The CLI finds the nearest `klenod.config.rb` by checking the current directory and walking up parent directories. It changes into the config directory before building.

      ```sh
      bundle exec klenod build
      ```

      You can inspect a built bundle without evaluating application modules by exporting Graphviz DOT.

      ```sh
      bundle exec klenod graph dist/klenod.bundle > graph.dot
      dot -Tsvg graph.dot > graph.svg
      ```

      Klenod's internal virtual modules are hidden by default. Use `--internal-virtual-modules` to show them. Application-facing virtual modules, such as the imported router, remain visible.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Runtime Loading" }
    :markdown
      Runtime bundles contain entrypoint mappings, module specs, import specs, runtime import values, asset specs, source maps, and source-root metadata. Runtime loading evaluates modules lazily.

      ```ruby
      require "klenod/runtime"

      bundle = Klenod::Runtime.load_bundle("dist/klenod.bundle")
      page = bundle.exports("entrypoint")
      ```

      Each entry in `bundle.modules` is a `Klenod::Runtime::ModuleSpec`. Its `transformed_hash` is the SHA256 digest of the transformed source, so deploy tooling can compare it across bundles to find changed modules.

      Call `bundle.preload_entrypoints` to eagerly evaluate everything reachable from configured entrypoints, or `bundle.preload` to evaluate every module in the bundle.

      Each loaded bundle owns its own runtime namespace. Multiple bundles can therefore run in one Ruby process even when they use the same module IDs. The namespace only isolates Klenod's generated modules; use Ruby::Box when bundles also need isolation from process-wide Ruby state.

      The default namespace is an anonymous module, so classes defined by bundled modules have names that Ruby cannot resolve, and Marshal cannot dump their instances. Pass a named module to give them resolvable names:

      ```ruby
      module MyApp::Modules
      end

      bundle = Klenod::Runtime.load_bundle("dist/klenod.bundle", namespace: MyApp::Modules)
      ```

      Generated constant names derive from module IDs, so they stay the same across builds. Bundles loaded into the same named namespace replace each other's generated modules when their module IDs overlap. Marshaling a bundle keeps a named namespace by reference; an anonymous namespace becomes a new anonymous module. `bundle.namespace = ...` changes the namespace after loading and discards already evaluated modules.

      Production deployments that only load prebuilt bundles should depend on `klenod-runtime`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Rack Helpers" }
    :markdown
      `klenod-rack` provides Rack-compatible helpers for serving runtime assets. It is an optional integration layer and does not define framework routing, rendering, or request dispatch policy.

